        optionFeedback: [`Chronic renal failure would present with progressive loss of kidney function and not evidence of acute inflammation or injury. It is a rare outcome of this disease in children as opposed to adults in who one-third of whom develop chronic renal disease.`,`The kidney disease is characteristic of an acute nephritic syndrome (microscopic hematuria and systemic manifestations of acute inflammation). The streptococcal infection preceding the renal disease and the presence of anti-streptococcal antibodies are consistent with acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis. The typical course of this disease in children is complete resolution within weeks or months. `,`The characteristic feature of acute renal failure is a sudden loss of renal function manifested by increased serum creatinine and often oliguria. It is a rare outcome in children with this disorder.`,`Rheumatic heart disease is also a sequel of streptococcal infection, but it affects the heart and not the kidneys. The streptococci that cause rheumatic fever are of different subtypes than those that cause renal disease. In addition, prompt antibiotic therapy usually prevents the development of rheumatic heart disease (but not glomerulonephritis).`,`Urinary tract infection manifests with local signs of inflammation (e.g., pain during urination and cloudy urine with bacteria and leukocytes). Streptococcus is not a known cause of urinary tract infection. `,``],
