        optionFeedback: [`The acute onset of hematuria and evidence of renal failure suggest nephritis, and the immunofluorescence staining pattern is indicative of the deposition of anti-GBM antibodies. This individual most likely has a form of RPGN. In severe cases, removal of the antibodies from the blood by plasmapheresis is required to alleviate the acute renal failure. Chronic immunosuppression will be needed to suppress the autoantibody production `,`The lesion is not caused by an infection and is not diagnostic of acute postinfectious GN, which is characterized by immune complex deposition in a granular immunofluorescence pattern. Antibiotics would not help even in postinfectious GN because the infection would have been in the past, and the renal lesions are not caused by ongoing infection. `,`Anti-TNF therapy is helpful for rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, and other autoimmune diseases in which T cell-mediated inflammation is prominent.`,`Hepatitis B vaccine is preventive, not therapeutic. Renal involvement is not common in hepatitis; when it occurs, it is caused by deposition of immune complexes in a granular pattern. `,`Infusion of HIV antibody has been used to treat rare cases of HIV infection but is not standard therapy. HIV-associated nephropathy is not associated with linear deposition of anti-GBM antibodies. `,``],
