        optionFeedback: [`Anti-GBM antibodies are deposited in a linear pattern and patients develop rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.`,`Anti-streptococcal antibodies form immune complexes that may be deposited in glomeruli. Antibodies that cross-react with streptococci are usually reactive with myocardial antigens and are the cause of rheumatic fever.`,`There are no known examples of renal disease caused by cytotoxic T lymphocytes (CTLs) reacting against renal antigens.`,`The immunofluorescence staining pattern is diagnostic of immune complex deposition, with subsequent activation of complement at the sites of deposition. The clinical presentation is that of nephrotic syndrome (proteinuria) with mild renal failure developing. This presentation and the lack of therapeutic response to steroids are characteristic of membranous nephropathy.`, `Prostaglandins are among the inflammatory mediators produced by numerous cell types during acute inflammatory reactions. They do not, by themselves, lead to immune complex deposition; rather, their production may be part of the inflammation that is induced by these complexes.`,``],
