        optionFeedback: [`Diffuse basement thickening is caused by the deposition of immune complexes in the GBM, and is characteristic of membranous nephropathy. This disease also causes the nephrotic syndrome, but it is more common in adults and much less responsive to steroid therapy.`,`The clinical presentation is of the nephrotic syndrome. In children, the most common cause is minimal change disease, which is characterized by normal glomerular histology (as the name of the disease implies). This disease also responds well to steroid therapy. The “selective” proteinuria (preferential loss of small proteins) distinguishes this disease from most other causes of the nephrotic syndrome, in which larger proteins are often lost.`,`Diffuse glomerular hypercellularity is a reflection of inflammation and proliferation of intrinsic glomerular cells. It is most often seen in postinfectious GN and lupus nephritis, both caused by extensive immune complex deposition in the glomerulus.`,`Focal sclerosis is seen in FSGS, which also causes the nephrotic syndrome but is more common in adults and more severe and in some cases leads to renal failure. The glomerular histology is abnormal.`,`Hyalinization is the result of end-stage glomerular disease, in which damaged glomeruli are replaced by extracellular matrix (ECM) material resembling glycosaminoglycans.`,``],
