        q: `A 44-year-old man with increasing malaise for the past month is now bothered by increasing swelling of his hands and legs. On physical examination, there is generalized edema. Urinalysis shows 4+ proteinuria and no blood, glucose, or ketones. Microscopic examination of the urine shows no casts or RBCs and 2 WBCs per high-power field. The 24-hour urine protein level is 4.2 g. A renal biopsy specimen is obtained, and immunofluorescence staining with an antibody against the C3 component of complement produces the pattern shown below. What is the most likely mechanism of proteinuria? `,
