        optionFeedback: [`Anti-GBM antibodies typically cause rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis characterized by hematuria and some proteinuria, usually not severe enough to cause nephrotic syndrome. Anti-GBM antibodies cause linear patterns of staining.`,`The history is not typical for HIV, and HIV-associated renal involvement usually presents as FSGS.`,`The clinical presentation (generalized edema caused by loss of protein in the urine) is typical for the nephrotic syndrome, and the granular staining pattern is diagnostic for membranous nephropathy. Immune complexes deposited in the glomeruli disrupt glomerular permeability by damaging podocytes and the GBM.`,`Mutations affecting basement membrane collagen are the cause of hereditary nephritis (Alport syndrome), which typically presents with hematuria and does not show immune complex deposition.`,`There are no known diseases caused by autoantibodies against C3. C3 glomerulopathies are caused by excessive C3 deposition resulting from defects in the regulation of complement pathways. In some cases this is a consequence of autoantibodies against C3 convertase which allow uncontrolled complement activation by the alternative pathway.`,``],
