        optionFeedback: [`antibodies reactive with centromeres and DNA topoisomerase are more common in systemic sclerosis.`,`antibodies reactive with centromeres and DNA topoisomerase are more common in systemic sclerosis.`,`This presentation of a systemic illness with arthritis and pleuritis is characteristic of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). Laboratory findings that support this diagnosis include reduced serum albumin (caused by proteinuria), increased creatinine mild renal failure), reduced C3 (complement consumption), and pancytopenias (antibody-mediated destruction of blood cells). The renal biopsy shows the features of the most common and severe form of lupus nephritis. The specific diagnostic test for SLE is the presence of antibodies against double-stranded DNA.`,`Anti-GBM antibodies are seen in cases of GN with linear antibody deposition; the most common clinical presentation is rapidly progressive GN (crescentic GN).`,`Antihistone antibodies may be present in SLE but they are more common in drug-induced lupus and are also found in other autoimmune diseases, so they are not as specific as anti-dsDNA.`, ``],
