        option: [`Antinuclear antibody`,`Plasma renin`,`Renal biopsy`,`Urine culture`,`Abdominal CT scan`],
        optionFeedback: [`Anti-nuclear antibody is detected in SLE, which would present with nephrotic syndrome or nephritis and would not show leukocytes in the urine.`,`Plasma renin is used to determine if hypertension has a renal cause.`,`Renal biopsy is done in cases when the diagnosis cannot be made by less invasive tests. It is most often done in nephrotic or nephritic syndrome caused by glomerular lesions.`,`Fever and chills indicate systemic inflammation, and flank pain suggests a process involving the kidneys. The presence of leukocytes in the urine is suggestive of pyelonephritis. A urine culture should be performed to identify the causative organism (usually a coliform bacterial species) in order to initiate antibiotic therapy.`,`Abdominal CT scan would reveal masses but would not be informative for acute bacterial pyelonephritis. If ureteral obstruction is suspected as a predisposing cause, a CT scan may be helpful. `,``],
