        optionFeedback: [`Analgesic nephropathy presents with tubulointerstitial nephritis with papillary necrosis.`,`Granulomatous vasculitis presents with acute-onset renal failure and fibrinoid necrosis of the renal blood vessels.`,`Diabetic nephropathy also presents with nephrotic syndrome and glomerular hyaline deposits, but there are no deposits in the interstitium or vessels. There is, of course, evidence of diabetes. `,`membranous nephropathy is an immune complex glomerulonephritis that causes the nephrotic syndrome, there are no deposits in the interstitium or vessels.`,`The back pain, bone lesions and immunosuppressed state evidenced by recurrent infections, all are consistent with the diagnosis of myeloma, a tumor of plasma cells. Patients with myeloma may develop amyloidosis, which causes the nephrotic syndrome (manifested by proteinuria and reduced serum albumin); the deposits of amorphous pink material are characteristic, amyloid can be confirmed with a Congo red stain. Nephrotic syndrome is caused by glomerular deposits of amyloid impairing glomerular permeability.`,``],
