        option: [`Inactivation of <i>VHL</i> with overexpression of VEGF`,`Inactivation of <i>WT1</i>, with overexpression of genes involved in renal differentiation`,`Inactivation of <i>APC</i>, with overexpression of beta catenin`, `Inactivation of <i>RB</i> with loss of G1-S control `,`<i>PTEN</i>, with activation of the PI3K/AKT tyrosine kinases`],
        optionFeedback: [`The history of flank pain and hematuria is suspicious for renal cell carcinoma, and the morphology is characteristic (yellowish tumor because of lipid-filled cells, often with areas of hemorrhage and vascular invasion). A frequent genetic lesion in this tumor is deletion or inactivation of the VHL gene. The VHL protein is a ubiquitin ligase that is responsible for degradation of many proteins, including the transcription factor HIF-1. Increased levels of HIF lead to overproduction of growth factors, including VEGF, which promotes angiogenesis and tumor growth.`,`Inactivation of the <i>WT1</i> gene is associated with Wilms’ tumor, a tumor associated with abnormal differentiation of the renal blastema because of reduced production (not overexpression) of genes associated with differentiation. The tumor presumably develops because of over-proliferation of primordial cells.`, `Inactivation of <i>APC</i> is associated with carcinoma of the colon.`,`Mutational inactivation of <i>RB</i> occurs in retinoblastomas and osteogenic sarcomas. High risk HPVs can also cause functional inactivation of <i>RB</i>.`,`Mutations of PTEN, an inhibitor of PI3-kinase, are seen in many tumors but not renal cell carcinoma.`,``],
        optionStyleType: `st-upper-alpha`,
        answer: [0],
        ansText: `Rationale:-The history of flank pain and hematuria is suspicious for renal cell carcinoma, and the morphology is characteristic (yellowish tumor because of lipid-filled cells, often with areas of hemorrhage and vascular invasion). A frequent genetic lesion in this tumor is deletion or inactivation of the VHL  gene. The VHL protein is a ubiquitin ligase that is responsible for degradation of many proteins, including the transcription factor HIF-1α. Increased levels of HIF-1α lead to overproduction of growth factors, including VEGF, which promotes angiogenesis and tumor growth.  `,
        state: `notAnswered`,
        userAnswered: [],
